    "factsheet": [
        {
            "heading": "Tour Summary",
            "paragraphs": [
                "Westminster is where it all happened for poor King Charles.",
                "His trial, final walk and execution site are all here.  We will be visiting them all, and reliving the frightening sense of chaos, and of a country lurching out of control which pervaded the bloody civil war which precipitated his downfall.",
                "We are lucky in the large variety of memorials and reminders of that period, put up by sympathisers of both sides in that struggle, as a battle for remembrance and meaning has been played out, and, indeed continues to this day.  The conflict between King and People is etched into the very fabric here.",
                "On the way, we will also see a reminder of another beheaded English monarch.  An earlier tragic Queen who perhaps foreshadowed Charles' fate.",
                "The graceful palace building, outside of which he was killed, still remains to this day, and, while death was final for Charles I, the symbolic resurrection of the central idea his life - the God-given rights of a King - did happen after his death, and is symbolised by a magnificent statue, cast during his lifetime, which puts King Charles at the very centre of today's London, as he is to British history.",
                "We finally escape the noise and bustle of modern London into the calm of the National Portrait Gallery, where we can contemplate the King and the man in front of a superb contemporary portrait."
            ]
        },
        {
            "heading": "Practical Details",
            "paragraphs": [
                "The walking tour takes 90 minutes.",
                "The route would take about 25 minutes if walked without stops.",
                "Parts of the route are rather crowded, and there are some roads to cross, all at pedestrian crossings.",
                "We do finish in the National Portrait Gallery. This is free to enter after a brief bag check by the Gallery staff.  Toilets and a Cafe are available inside the Gallery."
            ]
        }
    ],
